# The sdist must be able to rebuild itself.
#
# `python -m build` produces the sdist first and then builds the wheel FROM that
# sdist, so anything `setup.py` reaches for has to be inside it. `pyproject.toml`,
# `setup.py` and the README named by `[project] readme` are added by setuptools
# itself; the build filter is not, because it is neither a package module nor
# package data. Without this line the sdist unpacks, `setup.py` fails to load
# `packaging/machina_sports_canonical/build.py`, and the wheel build dies in the
# backend — which is how this line came to be written rather than assumed.
#
# The release helper is the second and last line, and it is here for a different
# reason: the build does not need it, a rebuild *of the release* does.
# `release.py` is what makes the sdist reproducible — it builds with the release
# epoch and rewrites the builder's mtimes, ownership and umask out of the tar —
# so an sdist that omitted it would be an artefact nobody could rebuild
# byte-for-byte from itself. It is build support, not runtime: it is not a package
# module, it is not package data, and `tests/test_iptc_canonical_package.py`
# asserts it is absent from the wheel.
#
# Nothing else is added. The canonical modules and declared JSON resources reach
# the sdist through the package configuration in `pyproject.toml`, and a
# `recursive-include` over `tools/` would additionally sweep in the harness, the
# fixtures and the reports, none of which belong in a published artefact.
include packaging/machina_sports_canonical/build.py
include packaging/machina_sports_canonical/release.py

# THE TWO EXCLUSIONS BELOW ARE WHAT MAKES A RELEASE BUILD AND A PROOF BUILD THE
# SAME ARTEFACT. `setuptools`' sdist defaults sweep in every root `README*` and
# every `tests/test*.py` on their own. The proof suite builds from a staged copy
# holding only the declared packaging inputs, so it never saw either; the release
# workflow builds from a checkout root, so it got both — the templates
# repository's own README and all twenty IPTC suites, inside a published
# distribution. Two different artefacts with one version number, and the release
# checklist comparing the digest of the one nobody proved.
#
# `README.md` here is the repository's README, not the package's:
# `README-machina-sports-canonical.md` is named by `[project] readme` and is added
# by setuptools itself. Nothing about the declared readme or the description bytes
# changes.
exclude README.md
prune tests
